So anyway, so I see this as a natural implication of this story.

208
00:15:57,640 --> 00:16:02,640
So we get stupid economic policies because voters have stupid beliefs about economics,

209
00:16:02,640 --> 00:16:06,640
and they have these stupid beliefs because if they vote on the basis of stupid beliefs,

210
00:16:06,640 --> 00:16:08,640
what happens to them as individuals?

211
00:16:08,640 --> 00:16:11,640
The same thing that happens to someone with a PhD in economics.

212
00:16:11,640 --> 00:16:14,640
So pretty simple.

213
00:16:14,640 --> 00:16:15,640
All right.

214
00:16:15,640 --> 00:16:18,640
And let's see.

215
00:16:18,640 --> 00:16:23,640
So another example that I like on the effect of incentives on rationality.

216
00:16:23,640 --> 00:16:28,640
So the basic economic point that labor market regulations,

217
00:16:28,640 --> 00:16:32,640
or especially pro-labor labor market regulations, reduce employment,

218
00:16:32,640 --> 00:16:38,640
it seems very intuitive, seems very well supported,

219
00:16:38,640 --> 00:16:40,640
and yet there are many people in the world who say,

220
00:16:40,640 --> 00:16:43,640
yeah, it's just right-wing ideology, that's the stuff you're making up.

221
00:16:43,640 --> 00:16:45,640
But anyway, of course you believe that.

222
00:16:45,640 --> 00:16:49,640
And yet my favorite reply to people like this is, well,

223
00:16:49,640 --> 00:16:55,640
have you ever filled out a job market application that had a line for salary requirements?

224
00:16:55,640 --> 00:16:56,640
Likely.

225
00:16:56,640 --> 00:17:00,640
Did you put a million dollars per hour as your salary requirement?

226
00:17:00,640 --> 00:17:01,640
No.

227
00:17:01,640 --> 00:17:02,640
Why not?

228
00:17:02,640 --> 00:17:08,640
If you don't just take for granted that there's labor demand elasticity,

229
00:17:08,640 --> 00:17:11,640
why not just ask for as much money as possible?

230
00:17:11,640 --> 00:17:12,640
Why not?

231
00:17:12,640 --> 00:17:13,640
Sky's the limit.

232
00:17:13,640 --> 00:17:20,640
And to me this shows that everybody actually realizes on a deep level when the stakes are significant,

233
00:17:20,640 --> 00:17:22,640
when there's a cost to them.

234
00:17:22,640 --> 00:17:25,640
That is when people's actual apply their self-discipline.

235
00:17:25,640 --> 00:17:27,640
They might actually like to put in a million dollars an hour.

236
00:17:27,640 --> 00:17:28,640
That would be fun, right?

237
00:17:28,640 --> 00:17:29,640
It would be fun to fill out the application.

238
00:17:29,640 --> 00:17:31,640
Hire me because I am awesome.

239
00:17:31,640 --> 00:17:34,640
And what is your experience?

240
00:17:34,640 --> 00:17:35,640
I'm great at everything.

241
00:17:35,640 --> 00:17:37,640
And to spill the whole application out, full wishful thinking.

242
00:17:37,640 --> 00:17:43,640
But you recognize that if you say these things that are mentally gratifying to you,

243
00:17:43,640 --> 00:17:45,640
that they will have very high personal costs.

244
00:17:45,640 --> 00:17:47,640
And so almost no one says things like this.

245
00:17:47,640 --> 00:17:54,640
And instead you go and try to constrain what you're saying to be truthy or true.

246
00:17:54,640 --> 00:17:57,640
And on the other hand, if you were voting for policy, this is where you can say,

247
00:17:57,640 --> 00:18:02,640
where do you get this idea that employers will hire fewer people when you raise the wage?

248
00:18:02,640 --> 00:18:04,640
It's just your made-up stuff.

249
00:18:04,640 --> 00:18:07,640
It's like, is it made-up stuff to think that if you enforce pollution regulations

250
00:18:07,640 --> 00:18:10,640
with harsher fines that there will be less pollution?

251
00:18:10,640 --> 00:18:11,640
Is that just made up?

252
00:18:11,640 --> 00:18:15,640
Is that something where we only believe it after there's massive evidence in its favor?

253
00:18:15,640 --> 00:18:19,640
Evidence that you'll never even, that's just too busy to ever look at?

254
00:18:19,640 --> 00:18:20,640
No.

255
00:18:20,640 --> 00:18:21,640
It is a presumption.

256
00:18:21,640 --> 00:18:24,640
Of course demand curves slope down.

257
00:18:24,640 --> 00:18:28,640
And if you want to say anything more about it, yes, fine, look at the evidence.

258
00:18:28,640 --> 00:18:32,640
But to start with the assumption of elasticity is just what I want,

259
00:18:32,640 --> 00:18:35,640
that's pretty crazy.

260
00:18:35,640 --> 00:18:36,640
All right.

261
00:18:36,640 --> 00:18:37,640
Questions about this?

262
00:18:37,640 --> 00:18:38,640
No one happy?

263
00:18:38,640 --> 00:18:39,640
Okay.

264
00:18:39,640 --> 00:18:41,640
All right.

265
00:18:41,640 --> 00:18:46,640
Now, one approach you can take to rational rationality is the puritanical one,

266
00:18:46,640 --> 00:18:52,640
where you just sit there saying, these despicable people defiling their minds with lies.

267
00:18:52,640 --> 00:18:54,640
This makes me sick.

268
00:18:54,640 --> 00:18:55,640
Sinners.

269
00:18:55,640 --> 00:18:56,640
Reprobates.

270
00:18:56,640 --> 00:18:57,640
All right.

271
00:18:57,640 --> 00:18:58,640
I'll confess.

272
00:18:58,640 --> 00:18:59,640
Many times I have that reaction.

273
00:18:59,640 --> 00:19:04,640
But if we're being economists about it, then if they enjoy it,

274
00:19:04,640 --> 00:19:08,640
if they're willing to pay something for it, then it counts in welfare calculations.

275
00:19:08,640 --> 00:19:14,640
So, yeah, even the sickest, most twisted pleasures go into the cost benefit calculations.

276
00:19:14,640 --> 00:19:17,640
You know, someone enjoys watching frogs get stepped on.

277
00:19:17,640 --> 00:19:19,640
That goes into the calculation.

278
00:19:19,640 --> 00:19:22,640
They make other calculations that people don't like that.

279
00:19:22,640 --> 00:19:23,640
That'll go in too.

280
00:19:23,640 --> 00:19:28,640
But at least we put in the calculations for the numbers for the people who enjoy these things.

281
00:19:28,640 --> 00:19:29,640
All right.

282
00:19:29,640 --> 00:19:34,640
So, if we're doing Calder Hicks efficiency analysis, then we should count the consumption value.

283
00:19:34,640 --> 00:19:38,640
But this in no way implies an efficient outcome.

284
00:19:38,640 --> 00:19:39,640
And why?

285
00:19:39,640 --> 00:19:41,640
Well, just think about the logic of externalities.

286
00:19:41,640 --> 00:19:47,640
So if you say, look, air pollution is efficient because it's convenient for people to pollute the air.

287
00:19:47,640 --> 00:19:48,640
Not true, right?

288
00:19:48,640 --> 00:19:51,640
It is true that it's convenient to pollute the air.

289
00:19:51,640 --> 00:19:54,640
Where does the inefficiency come from?

290
00:19:54,640 --> 00:19:55,640
Other people.

291
00:19:55,640 --> 00:19:56,640
Other people.

292
00:19:56,640 --> 00:19:59,640
It's not convenient for you, but you're causing problems for everybody else.


